{{also|Learn}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|larn||Northumbrian}}
* {{alt|en|learne||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:lernen<ety:inh<ang:leornian>>}}
From {{inh|en|enm|lernen|t=to learn", also, "to teach"}}, from {{inh|en|ang|leornian|t=to learn", rarely also, "to teach}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*liʀnōn}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*lizaną}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro||*(le-)lóys-e}}, stative from the root {{m|ine-pro|*leys-|t=track, furrow, trace, trail}}.

The spelling with ⟨ea⟩ is of {{w|Early Modern English}} origin and reflects a lengthening of {{IPAchar|/ɛ~e/}} to {{IPAfont|/eː/}} before {{IPAchar|/rn/}} in Late Old English or Early Middle English, then later lowering to {{IPAchar|/ɛː/}}; the modern pronunciation reflects still later reshortening. The dialect form with {{IPAfont|/ɑː/}} reflects the Middle English pronunciation which either retained the short {{IPAchar|/ɛ/}} or reshortened it earlier, enabling the a development to {{IPAchar|/a/}} before {{IPAchar|/r/}} which yielded modern {{IPAfont|/ɑː/}}.

Cognate with {{cog|ofs|lernia}}, {{m|ofs|lerna|t=to learn}}, {{cog|gml|lernen|t=to learn", also, "to teach}}, {{cog|dum|leernen|t=to learn", also, "to teach}} (whence {{cog|nl|lernen|t=to study scripture}}), {{cog|de|lernen|t=to learn}}. See also {{m|en|lore}} and {{m|en|lear#Verb}}.

===Pronunciation===
